---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BOOKING_URL, COMPLIANCE } from '../site.config';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'Retirement Income Planning',
    description: 'Education on retirement income planning, Social Security timing, annuity income riders, and bucket strategies.',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: [
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What is the retirement income gap?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'The retirement income gap is the difference between the guaranteed income you can count on — like Social Security or a pension — and the actual expenses you expect to have in retirement. Closing that gap usually requires drawing on savings or building additional guaranteed income sources.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'Does it matter when I start Social Security?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Yes. Your monthly Social Security benefit is permanently reduced if you claim before your full retirement age, and permanently increased if you delay claiming past it, up to age 70. The right claiming age depends on your health, other income sources, and overall financial picture.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'What is a bucket strategy?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'A bucket strategy divides retirement savings into separate "buckets" based on when you plan to spend them — for example, a short-term bucket for the next 1-3 years of expenses, a mid-term bucket, and a long-term growth bucket. The goal is to avoid being forced to sell investments at a loss during a market downturn.',
        },
      },
      {
        '@type': 'Question',
        name: 'How can an annuity fit into a retirement income plan?',
        acceptedAnswer: {
          '@type': 'Answer',
          text: 'Some fixed indexed annuities offer optional income riders. Payments, fees, eligibility, and conditions depend on the contract and insurer; review the rider terms to understand what income may be available and when.',
        },
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://prosperityservicesgroup.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Retirement Income', item: 'https://prosperityservicesgroup.com/retirement-income' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Retirement Income Planning Guide | Prosperity Group"
  description="Explore retirement income planning, Social Security timing, annuity rider features, and bucket strategies in plain English."
  path="/retirement-income"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-gold">Retirement Income</p>
      <h1>Planning for Retirement Income</h1>
      <p class="subhead">
        Saving for retirement and generating income in retirement are two very different problems.
        Here's how to think through closing the gap between what you'll have coming in and what
        you'll need to spend.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container narrow">
      <h2>Why "how much did I save" isn't the whole question</h2>
      <p>
        Most of the financial industry, and most of our own habits, are built around a single
        question: how much have you saved? That question matters, but it isn't the whole picture
        once you're actually retired. Useful questions include how much income different approaches may
        produce, how predictable it is, and how long it may last. Two people can retire with the
        same account balance and have different outcomes depending on how it is held, when Social
        Security is claimed, and whether a portion is used to purchase an annuity contract.
      </p>

      <h2>The retirement income gap</h2>
      <p>
        Most retirees have some amount of guaranteed income — Social Security, and for some, a
        pension. But for many people, that guaranteed income doesn't fully cover expected
        expenses. The difference between guaranteed income and expected spending is often called
        the retirement income gap, and closing it usually means either drawing down savings,
        adjusting spending, or building additional guaranteed income sources to fill the space.
      </p>

      <h2>Social Security timing basics</h2>
      <p>
        One of the most impactful — and most overlooked — retirement income decisions is when to
        start Social Security. Claiming before your full retirement age permanently reduces your
        monthly benefit, while delaying past it, up to age 70, permanently increases it. The
        "right" answer depends on your health, life expectancy expectations, other income sources,
        and whether you're still working. For married couples, coordinating each spouse's claiming
        strategy can meaningfully change total lifetime income.
      </p>

      <h2>Annuity income riders</h2>
      <p>
        Some fixed indexed annuities offer optional income riders, often for an additional cost. A rider
        may provide payments under specified conditions, but the payout basis, fees, eligibility,
        income amount, and access to contract value depend on its terms. Guarantees depend on the
        insurer's claims-paying ability and the contract; they do not mean every essential expense
        will be covered. Review the full contract and rider details with a licensed agent. Learn more about
        how these riders work on our <a href="/income">Lifetime Income Planning</a> page and our
        <a href="/annuities">Fixed Indexed Annuities</a> page.
      </p>

      <h2>The bucket strategy</h2>
      <p>
        A bucket strategy is a common way to organize retirement savings around when you'll
        actually need the money, rather than treating your whole portfolio the same way:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-3">
        <div class="card card-gold">
          <h3>Short-Term Bucket</h3>
          <p>Cash and other relatively stable holdings intended for near-term expenses; this may reduce the need to sell other assets during a downturn, but does not eliminate risk.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-gold">
          <h3>Mid-Term Bucket</h3>
          <p>Lower-volatility assets — which can include a fixed indexed annuity — meant to refill the short-term bucket over the next several years.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card card-gold">
          <h3>Long-Term Bucket</h3>
          <p>Growth-oriented investments not needed for a decade or more, given room to ride out market ups and downs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        The goal of a bucket approach is to reduce "sequence of returns risk" — the danger of
        having to sell investments at a loss early in retirement, which can permanently damage how
        long your savings last.
      </p>

      <h2>Matching income sources to expenses</h2>
      <p>
        One helpful way to think about retirement income is to separate your expenses into
        essential costs — housing, utilities, food, health care premiums — and discretionary
        costs, like travel or hobbies. Many retirees find it reassuring to match essential expenses
        against recurring income sources such as Social Security, a pension, or annuity payments. Actual
        coverage depends on the amount and terms of each source; rider payments, if elected, depend
        on the contract and insurer. Investment account values can fluctuate, so a plan should
        account for the possibility that income may not cover every expense.
      </p>

      <h2>Common retirement income mistakes</h2>
      <p>
        A few patterns show up repeatedly in retirement income planning conversations: claiming
        Social Security at 62 without weighing the long-term reduction in benefits; withdrawing
        from investment accounts on autopilot regardless of market conditions, which increases
        sequence of returns risk; and failing to account for how required minimum distributions or
        taxes might affect the income you actually keep. Each of these is avoidable with some
        upfront planning, which is exactly the kind of thing a written retirement income plan is
        designed to catch before it becomes a problem.
      </p>

      <h2>Building your plan</h2>
      <p>
        The right combination of Social Security timing, guaranteed income products, and
        investment buckets depends entirely on your specific goals, expenses, and other assets. A
        licensed insurance agent can walk through your full financial picture — including how a
        <a href="/annuities">fixed indexed annuity</a> or <a href="/long-term-care">long-term care</a>
        plan might fit alongside your other savings — and help identify where guaranteed income
        fits best for your situation.
      </p>

      <div class="disclaimer">
        {COMPLIANCE.annuityDisclaimer}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt cta-band">
    <div class="container cta-inner">
      <h2>Ready to map out your retirement income?</h2>
      <p>Schedule a free, no-pressure consultation with a licensed insurance agent.</p>
      <a href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-gold">Schedule a Call</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero { background: var(--color-lavender); padding: 4rem 0; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .page-hero { background: var(--color-bg-alt); }
  .subhead { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 1.1rem; max-width: 700px; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .narrow { max-width: 820px; }
  .cta-band { text-align: center; }
  .cta-inner p { color: var(--color-text-muted); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
</style>
